# Automated Equity Research Engine v0.4

This version turns the discovery system into an end-to-end research funnel:

```text
Broad U.S. stock universe
        ↓
Persistent incremental price history
        ↓
Discovery
        ↓
Cached fundamentals + analyst revisions
        ↓
Cached news + SEC 10-K / 10-Q / 8-K
        ↓
Company-type classification
        ↓
Bear / Base / Bull valuation
        ↓
Expected return FROM TODAY'S PRICE
        ↓
BUY / SMALL BUY / WATCH / TOO LATE / PASS
```

A stock is **not excluded merely because it already rose**. A late-stage stock
can still earn BUY if the model sees enough forward return from today's price.
Likewise, an unfamiliar discovery candidate must survive valuation and risk
analysis before it earns BUY.

## Persistent data

`data/warehouse.db` stores daily prices, cached Yahoo data, cached news,
immutable SEC filings, and research reports.

Default refresh TTLs:

```text
market prices      14h (incremental update)
profile            48h
financials        120h
analyst data       16h
news                8h
SEC submissions    12h
SEC filing text    immutable after accession is stored
```

The first run is expensive. Later runs update only missing/stale data.

Check it with:

```bash
inflection-scanner cache-status
```

## Local quick test

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
pip install -e ".[dashboard,dev,llm]"

inflection-scanner doctor --network
pytest -q

inflection-scanner research \
  --max-universe 500 \
  --deep 30 \
  --research-count 10 \
  --top 15
```

Full run:

```bash
inflection-scanner research \
  --deep 100 \
  --research-count 20 \
  --top 30
```

Explain one name:

```bash
inflection-scanner explain TICKER
```

Test using only cached data:

```bash
inflection-scanner research \
  --offline \
  --max-universe 500 \
  --deep 30 \
  --research-count 10
```

## Negative P/E

Negative forward P/E is treated as **N/A**, not as a cheap valuation signal.

## Valuation specialists

The engine classifies companies as:

- PROFITABLE_GROWTH
- CYCLICAL
- EARLY_STAGE_GROWTH
- TURNAROUND
- GENERAL
- SPECIAL_CASE

Profitable companies use forward EPS scenario valuation. Cyclicals use
normalized positive annual EPS so peak earnings are not blindly capitalized.
Unprofitable growth companies can use forward revenue. Financials/real estate
are intentionally marked SPECIAL_CASE when a generic model is not reliable.

## SEC setup

Local `.env`:

```env
SEC_USER_AGENT=Your Name your.email@example.com
```

GitHub Actions secret:

```text
SEC_USER_AGENT
```

Without it, the rest of the engine runs but SEC-document research is skipped.

## Optional OpenAI evidence summary

The deterministic engine makes the BUY/WATCH/PASS decision. If
`OPENAI_API_KEY` is present, the program additionally uses the OpenAI Responses
API to summarize only the evidence already collected. It does not override the
deterministic decision.

Optional GitHub secret:

```text
OPENAI_API_KEY
```

Optional repository variable:

```text
OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5-mini
```

## GitHub Actions

Main workflow:

```text
.github/workflows/research.yml
```

It runs on weekdays and can also be started manually from the GitHub Actions UI.

It:

1. restores `data/warehouse.db` from GitHub Actions cache
2. runs the research engine
3. refreshes only stale/missing data
4. saves the updated warehouse back to cache
5. uploads the full run as a downloadable artifact
6. commits only the small `published/` dashboard payload back to the repo

The large binary warehouse is deliberately **not** committed to Git.

## Streamlit

The Streamlit dashboard reads only:

```text
published/latest_research.json
published/metadata.json
```

Run locally:

```bash
streamlit run dashboard/app.py
```

For hosted use, deploy `dashboard/app.py` on Streamlit Community Cloud from the
same GitHub repository. GitHub Actions does the expensive research; Streamlit
is a lightweight read-only dashboard.

## Decision columns

The dashboard shows:

- BUY / SMALL BUY / WATCH / TOO LATE / PASS
- confidence
- current price
- scenario-weighted 3-year value
- expected 3-year CAGR
- scenario probability of profit
- bear-case downside
- current price maturity
- company type / valuation model
- filing evidence
- recent cached news
- why buy / why not / what changes the decision

The scenario probabilities are model assumptions, not statistically calibrated
probabilities. Treat them as an auditable decision framework, not a guarantee.
